------------------------------------------------------
Quick Guide To Patching This Package For The Impatient
------------------------------------------------------

1. Make sure you have quilt installed
2. Unpack the package as usual with "dpkg-source -x"
3. Run the "patch" target in debian/rules
4. Create a new patch with "quilt new" (see quilt(1))
5. Edit all the files you want to include in the patch with "quilt edit" 
   (see quilt(1)).
6. Write the patch with "quilt refresh" (see quilt(1))
7. Run the "clean" target in debian/rules

Alternatively, instead of using quilt directly, you can drop the patch in to 
debian/patches and add the name of the patch to debian/patches/series.


The X Strike Force team maintains X packages in git repositories on
salsa.debian.org in the xorg-team subdirectory. Most upstream packages
are actually maintained in git repositories as well, so they often
just need to be pulled into salsa.debian.org in a "upstream-*" branch.

The .orig.tar.gz is upstream's mesa tarball.

While building from git, dpkg-source can complain about symlinks vs.
normal files mismatches. To work around this issue, before uploading,
and without committing:

  find -type l | while read dest; do src=$(readlink -f $dest); rm $dest; cp $src $dest; done

The Debian packaging is added by creating the "debian-*" git branch
which contains the aforementioned "upstream-*" branch plus the debian/
repository files.
When a patch has to be applied to the Debian package, two solutions
are involved:
* If the patch is available in one of the upstream branches, it
  may be git'cherry-picked into the Debian repository. In this
  case, it appears directly in the .diff.gz.
* Otherwise, the patch is added to debian/patches/ which is managed
  with quilt as documented in /usr/share/doc/quilt/README.source.
  Thus, the patching system requires a build dependency on quilt.

Updating debian/upstream/signing-key.asc with new keys requires the following steps:
* fetch the key:
  gpg [--keyserver $server] --recv-keys $keyid
* export it to the file:
  gpg --export-options export-minimal --export -a $keyid >> debian/upstream/signing-key.asc

Building from Mesa 26.3
----------------------

This package is built from the Mesa staging/26.3 branch (26.3.0). The
upstream source is a plain "git archive" of the selected commit; debian/
is kept separately.

The package version uses the Debian "+really" convention:

  27.0.0~devel-9+really26.3.0-1

This sorts above the 27.0.0~devel-9 packages that users may already have
installed, while a future 27.0.0-1 sorts above it again.  No epoch is used.
debian/rules strips the "+really..." prefix before writing VERSION, so Mesa
itself still reports 26.3.0.

Because this version is still lower than 27.0.0~devel-10-1, do not publish
newer 27.0.0~devel-* packages in the same repository; apt would prefer those
instead.  For later 26.3.x uploads keep the same carrier prefix and update
the real version, for example 27.0.0~devel-9+really26.3.1-1.

The build intentionally fetches Rust tooling and crates at configure
time instead of declaring them as Build-Depends:

* bindgen-cli is installed with "cargo install" into debian/toolchain.
* cbindgen 0.28.0 is installed the same way, and its directory is put
  before /usr/bin in PATH so it wins over older distro cbindgen packages.
* The *-rs.wrap subprojects are downloaded by Meson. debhelper normally
  passes --wrap-mode=nodownload, so debian/rules appends
  --wrap-mode=default after it (Meson's last option wins).
* venus-protocol.wrap is a [wrap-git] wrap, so Meson clones it with
  "git" instead of downloading a tarball; git is therefore a real
  Build-Depends. It is only used by the "virtio" Vulkan driver
  (venus), which Debian enables on the LLVM architectures.

Therefore the build requires network access to crates.io, to
gitlab.freedesktop.org (venus-protocol) and to the source URLs listed in
subprojects/*.wrap. Before generating a source package run
"debian/rules purge-subprojects" to remove the downloaded subproject
tree and cache.
